---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Section Summary: Before the Code</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-4" />

        <p class="text-secondary">
          Activities 1.1 &ndash; 1.3. Use this to review, or to fill in a definition you missed.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">1.</span> What a Program Is</h2>

          <p>
            A program is instructions carried out in
            order. That is the whole idea, and everything else this year is
            built on it.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Program</dt>
            <dd>
            A list of instructions carried out in order, one at a time.
            </dd>
            <dt>Statement</dt>
            <dd>A single instruction in a program.</dd>
            <dt>Output</dt>
            <dd>Information a program produces.</dd>
            <dt>Create Performance Task</dt>
            <dd>
            A program you build during class that counts for 30% of your
            AP score.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">2.</span> Data and What It Costs</h2>

          <p>
            Underneath every file is a pile of
            bits. Compression makes that pile smaller, and the choice of method
            decides whether you can get the original back.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Bit</dt>
            <dd>A single 0 or 1. The smallest piece of data there is.</dd>
            <dt>Byte</dt>
            <dd>Eight bits, which can represent 256 different values.</dd>
            <dt>Compression</dt>
            <dd>Storing the same information in less space.</dd>
            <dt>Lossless</dt>
            <dd>Compression you can fully undo.</dd>
            <dt>Lossy</dt>
            <dd>Compression that permanently discards some data.</dd>
            <dt>Metadata</dt>
            <dd>Data about data &mdash; when, where, and how a file was made.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">3.</span> Who Computing Helps, and Who It Hurts</h2>

          <p>
            Every computing
            innovation has effects its creators did not plan. Asking who is
            left out, and where the bias came from, is part of the
            work.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Computing innovation</dt>
            <dd>Anything that includes a program as part of how it works.</dd>
            <dt>Digital divide</dt>
            <dd>
            The gap in access to computing between groups of people.
            </dd>
            <dt>Bias</dt>
            <dd>
            A systematic unfairness built into data, design, or use.
            </dd>
            <dt>Crowdsourcing</dt>
            <dd>Gathering many small contributions from many people.</dd>
            <dt>License</dt>
            <dd>
            A statement from an owner about what others may do with
            their work.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  dl {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  dt {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  dd {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }
  section {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
